# rustdoc

Sources: [rustdoc book](https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/rustdoc/how-to-write-documentation.html), RFC 505 / 1574, API Guidelines C-EXAMPLE, C-QUESTION-MARK, C-FAILURE, C-LINK.

- English. `//!` only for crate/module docs. `///` on items. Line comments, not `/** */`.
- rustdoc says what the item does in gprx / GPR terms. Do not define an item by another product's name or API (`.cursor/rules/rust-api.mdc`).
- First line: one punctuated sentence, third-person singular (“Returns…”, not “Return…”). rustdoc uses it as the search summary.
- Then: why/when, then sections. Common headings (plural `Examples` even for one): `# Examples`, `# Errors`, `# Panics`, `# Safety`.
- `# Errors` on every public `Result`. `# Panics` only if it can panic (gprx library paths should not). `# Safety` on every `unsafe`.
- Public user-facing types and methods need an example, or a link to one (C-EXAMPLE “within reason”). Getters that only return a field can skip a code block.
- Doc tests compile. Use `?`, not `unwrap`. Hide setup with `# ` lines. Fence with ` ```rust `.
- Link types with `[`Gpr`]`. Do not restate the signature; rustdoc already shows it.
- Do not document “this field is ignored when…”. If that sentence seems needed, the types are wrong (`.cursor/rules/types.mdc`).
- `missing_docs` deny on the lib crate (M0-1). `pub(crate)` items do not need rustdoc. `#[doc(hidden)]` for impls users cannot name (C-HIDDEN).
- Crate-level `//!` in `lib.rs` (1b): one-line role, then a copy-paste fit→predict example.

```rust
/// Returns the predictive mean and variance at `xs`.
///
/// Applies trained input transforms, then inverse-transforms the target.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns [`GprError::NotFitted`] if [`Gpr::fit`] has not succeeded.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```rust
/// # fn main() -> Result<(), gprx::GprError> {
/// let pred = model.predict(&xs)?;
/// # Ok(())
/// # }
/// ```
```
